with all these intense scenes what was  the atmosphere that was created on set  to make this happen  our director Andrew Neil did a really  good job of creating a safe environment  upset you know we went as far as to have  a safe word which I feel like was  important some of these moments were so  so heavy we only had to use safe word  once but the environment was very safe  you know and but also not not being too  fragile with it I think that there was  almost you know there was a lot of  Andrew pulling the the fraternity  brothers aside and the pledges were in a  different room you know so that he  wanted that fear to be there a little  bit and for it to be slightly  uncomfortable in moments but we always  knew that we had a safety net that was  never going to go to a point where  anyone felt you know like they weren't  safe if they weren't comfortable so it  was a good environment so how did you  guys blow off steam the of these intense  scenes was what what did you do - for  fun to lighten things up after these  intense scenes after some of these  intense scenes you know we were really  limited on what we could do speed so  little time to shoot it but you know a  beer after shooting and kind of  decompressing all of us just talking and  you know there was a great feeling  amongst the boys that we needed to  support each other outside of the set  and so yeah a few nights with a beer and  a cigar and just kind of kick him back  and trying to relax a little bit  and just talking you know you need to  sort of work through it a little bit  yeah does this affect your music career  and for instance did this experience  inspire you to write any new material  this experience was inspiring in a lot  of ways you know I think the the biggest  was was just going to a different  artistic and creative place being  challenged by you know other actors in  the director just pushed me to push  myself harder you know and dig deep and  and write things that that you know were  as gripping to me as the the film work  was you know so I think your art has to  when you have  couple different is going on so kind of  go at the same level parallel or that  imbalance can be a little crazy but it  was nice to to be able to blow off steam  by just going and writing you know  creating doing something different  because this film did take a lot out of  me  in what way uh it was just an emotional  roller coaster you know six weeks of  just like really being in the trenches  you need a little bit of fun afterwards  and just kind of spaced out a little bit  James Franco anything surprising from  working with him well I'm not sure I was  too surprised I kind of expected the  experience to be really unique and  interesting but he came in and was just  guns blazing just on fire and kind of  blew all of our minds and his character  is this sort of legacy fraternity  brother who comes back and says he's  gonna have one drink and cut to not one  drink but it was it was funny I think  we're supposed to be in the scene  incredibly fascinated by this guy and  you know thinking he's the man and it  wasn't too far off from reality I think  well all those young men and actors were  just so excited to be in the room with  with James and also his creative input  on the film as a producer it was great  and you know overall it was just a  really amazing experience
 

